# Agent 5 contribution: causal, graph-based, and robust HARMONIC extension

## Central gap and proposed idea

HARMONIC separates strategic cognition from tactical control and requires explanation, memory, and teamwork, but it does not by itself specify a testable representation that links observations to beliefs, goals, teammate models, actions, outcomes, and explanations. Language-model explanations can be fluent yet post-hoc: they may not identify the actual cause of an action, expose uncertainty, or remain valid after a sensor failure, misleading instruction, or novel environment. The proposed extension is an **Uncertainty-Aware Causal Interaction Graph (U-CIG)** shared between the strategic and tactical layers. It stores time-indexed nodes for observations, entities, goals, beliefs/intentions, candidate actions, execution outcomes, disturbances, and explanations; typed edges represent perceptual evidence, temporal dependence, belief-of-teammate, precondition/effect, and causal support.

The robot learns/updates a structural causal model over this graph and produces explanations from the graph's action trace rather than generating free-form rationales. A counterfactual explanation answers: “I selected action A because evidence E changed belief B and A best advanced goal G; if E were absent or the alternative teammate intention were true, I would have selected A'.” Explanations include confidence, affected goal, rejected alternatives, and the observation or intervention that would change the decision.

## Robustness and causal validation

Train the graph encoder and policy with uncertainty-aware contrastive objectives, masked-node reconstruction, and adversarial/domain-randomized episodes. Perturbations should include camera blur/occlusion, GPS drift, dropped or delayed messages, altered object appearance, contradictory natural-language requests, deceptive teammate reports, and unseen layouts/tasks. Calibrate both node uncertainty and action confidence; defer to a teammate or request clarification when uncertainty exceeds a safety threshold.

Do not treat explanation quality as text similarity. Validate causal faithfulness by interventions: remove or edit a claimed evidence node, change a goal/precondition, or replay the same state with a counterfactual teammate belief. The predicted action and the explanation's stated reason should change exactly when the intervened variable is causally relevant. Compare graph-derived explanations with plausible LLM rationales using deletion/insertion tests, counterfactual consistency, intervention agreement, and human judgments of usefulness and trust. A held-out causal-discovery test can measure whether learned edge directions recover known simulator dependencies under confounding and partial observability.

## Experimental proposal

Use a procedurally generated multi-robot search-and-retrieval benchmark patterned after HARMONIC's UGV-plus-drone lost-keys scenario. Each episode varies map topology, weather/lighting, target locations, robot capabilities, communication bandwidth, teammate policy, and task language. Include train/validation/test splits by map, object appearance, task composition, disturbance type, and teammate policy, with a fully held-out combination split. If feasible, transfer a subset to a UGV and quadrotor or a wheeled robot plus simulated drone.

Baselines: a flat LLM/VLM planner; a VLA policy with textual rationale; HARMONIC-style strategic/tactical integration without U-CIG; a symbolic planner with hand-authored explanations; and an ablated U-CIG without uncertainty, causal edges, counterfactual training, or adversarial augmentation. Report task success, time/energy, path length, communication turns/bandwidth, intervention recovery, and safety violations. Report OOD and adversarial degradation, calibration (ECE/Brier and selective risk-coverage), graph edge precision/recall against simulator ground truth, explanation faithfulness (action agreement after interventions, deletion/insertion, counterfactual validity), human-rated correctness/usefulness/trust, and strategic-to-tactical latency.

Hypotheses: (H1) U-CIG improves OOD success and disturbance recovery over flat language/VLA baselines; (H2) uncertainty-aware deferral reduces unsafe actions and improves calibration at modest communication cost; (H3) causal graph explanations have higher intervention agreement and human trust than fluent post-hoc rationales; (H4) removing causal edges or counterfactual training disproportionately harms novel disturbances and teammate-belief changes.

## Why prior work is insufficient

Existing explainable planners often expose symbolic plans but omit learned perception uncertainty, teammate mental states, and low-level execution failures. LLM/VLM/VLA systems improve language-grounded control but explanations are generally generated after the decision and are not guaranteed faithful. Standard robustness benchmarks focus on recognition or single-robot action success, not coupled perception, communication, belief update, and explanation under interventions. U-CIG makes these claims measurable through graph ablations and causal interventions while retaining HARMONIC's modular strategic/tactical interface.
